<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orthez</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Orthez is a town on the Gave de Pau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees">Pyrénées-Atlantiques</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It became the capital of Béarn under the viscounts of the Moncade line in the 13th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-iii-of-foix">Gaston Fébus</a> held his court in its Moncade castle in the 14th. Its fortified medieval bridge survives, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> defeated Soult nearby in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orthez.jpg" alt="Orthez" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orthez_Pont.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Orthez is a commune in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees">Pyrénées-Atlantiques</a> department, in the region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It lies 40 kilometres north-west of Pau on the railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> and straddles the westward-flowing Gave de Pau, with most of the town proper on the right bank. Its Basque name is Ortheze and its Occitan name Ortès. The town also includes the small village of Sainte-Suzanne, an independent commune until 1973, and has rail connections to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbes">Tarbes</a>, Pau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and Bayonne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Orthez was a capital of Béarn, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morlaas">Morlaàs</a> and before Pau. At the end of the twelfth century it passed from the viscounts of Dax to the viscounts of Béarn, whose chief residence it became in the thirteenth century. Froissart records the splendour of the court of Orthez under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-iii-of-foix">Gaston Phoebus</a> in the latter half of the fourteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeanne-dalbret">Jeanne d'Albret</a> founded a Calvinist university in the town, where Theodore Beza taught for some time.</p><p>An envoy sent by Charles IX in 1569 to revive the Catholic faith had to stand a siege in the battle of Orthez, and the town was taken by assault by the Huguenot captain Gabriel, count of Montgomery. In 1684 the intendant Nicholas Foucault, acting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, was more successful, as the inhabitants at least ostensibly renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, which remains a strong tradition in the town. A second battle of Orthez took place on 27 February 1814, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a> defeated Marshal Soult on the hills north of the town. The physicist Gaston Planté, born in Orthez on 22 April 1834, invented the lead-acid battery in 1859. Orthez was the seat of a subprefecture from 1800 until 1926.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Gave de Pau is crossed by a fourteenth-century bridge with four arches, topped at its centre by a tower. The Tour Moncade, a pentagonal tower of the thirteenth century, was once the keep of a castle of the viscounts of Béarn and is now a meteorological observatory. A church dating from the twelfth, fourteenth and fifteenth centuries and a seventeenth-century building, the remains of the Calvinist university, also survive.</p><p>Early in the twentieth century industries included the weaving of hemp and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a>, flour-milling, paper and leather, and the preparation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> hams. The basketball club Élan Béarnais won the Korać Cup in 1984, and the club moved to Pau in 1991. Natives include Gaston III Febus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeanne-dalbret">Jeanne d'Albret</a>, the poet Francis Jammes and the geographer Onésime Reclus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Orthez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1290 the counts inherited Béarn, and Gaston III Fébus ruled both from <b>Orthez</b> as a near-sovereign prince and wrote a famous hunting book.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-foix">County of Foix</a></li><li><blockquote>The seat later moved to <b>Orthez</b> and, from 1464, to Pau.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-bearn">Viscounty of Béarn</a></li></ul></section>
